systemd: recover a namespace fd's type from its nsfs name when NS_GET_NSTYPE is missing

systemd-machine-id-commit.service is one of the units still failing after
attempt 8.  It exits 1, and running its ExecStart by hand with
SYSTEMD_LOG_LEVEL=debug names the cause exactly:

  # SYSTEMD_LOG_LEVEL=debug systemd-machine-id-setup --commit
  Unmounting '/etc/machine-id'...
  Failed to switch back to initial mount namespace: Inappropriate ioctl for device
  We'll keep transient /etc/machine-id file until next reboot.

"Inappropriate ioctl for device" is ENOTTY.  machine_id_commit() does its work
inside a private mount namespace and then calls namespace_enter() to return to
the original one; that path reaches is_our_namespace() and then
fd_is_namespace(), which identifies an fd in two steps.  The first,
fd_is_fs_type(fd, NSFS_MAGIC), works here.  The second is
ioctl(fd, NS_GET_NSTYPE), and that ioctl is Linux 4.11 and later.  This kernel
is 4.9.190, so it answers ENOTTY and the commit is abandoned.

The consequence is quiet, which is why it is worth spelling out: the tool still
exits 0, only the unit fails, and /etc/machine-id stays a tmpfs bind mount.  The
machine id is therefore regenerated on every single boot.  Anything keyed to it
-- journal namespacing, systemd-random-seed credit, and anything that later
wants a stable identity for this device -- silently gets a new identity each
time, with no error to point at.

The type is recoverable without the ioctl.  nsfs names its inodes
"<type>:[<inode>]", the same strings namespace_info[].proc_name already holds,
and it has done so since namespaces were first exposed under /proc/$PID/ns --
long before NS_GET_NSTYPE existed.  Confirmed on the device:

  # for n in mnt pid net user uts ipc; do printf "%-6s -> " $n; readlink /proc/self/ns/$n; done
  mnt    -> mnt:[4026531840]
  pid    -> pid:[4026531836]
  net    -> net:[4026531910]
  user   -> user:[4026531837]
  uts    -> uts:[4026531838]
  ipc    -> ipc:[4026531839]

and through an fd, which is the shape fd_is_namespace() actually has:

  # sh -c 'exec 9</proc/self/ns/mnt; readlink /proc/self/fd/9'
  mnt:[4026531840]

This is the same move patch 0011 made for mount ids: when the new dedicated
interface is absent, read the answer out of procfs, which has carried it all
along.

Only the ENOTTY path changes.  ERRNO_IS_IOCTL_NOT_SUPPORTED() rather than a bare
ENOTTY comparison because it is what systemd already uses for unsupported
ioctls, and it also covers the EINVAL that some kernels return instead.  A
kernel with the ioctl never reaches the fallback, and any other ioctl error is
still propagated.  fd_is_fs_type() has already established the fd is nsfs before
either path runs, so a name that does not match the requested type means the
wrong namespace type, not a bad fd -- which is the false that fd_is_namespace()
already returns for that case.
--- a/src/basic/namespace-util.c
+++ b/src/basic/namespace-util.c
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
 #include "errno-util.h"
 #include "fd-util.h"
 #include "fileio.h"
+#include "fs-util.h"
 #include "log.h"
 #include "mountpoint-util.h"
 #include "namespace-util.h"
@@ -22,6 +23,7 @@
 #include "process-util.h"
 #include "stat-util.h"
 #include "stdio-util.h"
+#include "string-util.h"
 #include "uid-range.h"
 #include "unaligned.h"
 #include "user-util.h"
@@ -329,6 +331,30 @@
         return 0;
 }
 
+static int fd_is_namespace_by_name(int fd, NamespaceType type) {
+        _cleanup_free_ char *name = NULL;
+        int r;
+
+        assert(fd >= 0);
+        assert(type >= 0 && type < _NAMESPACE_TYPE_MAX);
+
+        /* Fallback for NS_GET_NSTYPE, which is Linux 4.11 and later.  nsfs names every inode it
+         * hands out "<type>:[<inode>]", and those type strings are exactly the proc_name field of
+         * namespace_info[].  That naming predates the ioctl -- it is how /proc/$PID/ns entries have
+         * read since namespaces were first exposed -- so reading the link back recovers the answer
+         * the ioctl would have given, without asking the kernel for anything it does not have. */
+
+        r = readlink_malloc(FORMAT_PROC_FD_PATH(fd), &name);
+        if (r < 0)
+                return r;
+
+        const char *rest = startswith(name, namespace_info[type].proc_name);
+        if (!rest)
+                return false; /* nsfs, but some other namespace type */
+
+        return rest[0] == ':';
+}
+
 int fd_is_namespace(int fd, NamespaceType type) {
         int r;
 
@@ -345,8 +371,12 @@
                 return true;
 
         int clone_flag = ioctl(fd, NS_GET_NSTYPE);
-        if (clone_flag < 0)
+        if (clone_flag < 0) {
+                if (ERRNO_IS_IOCTL_NOT_SUPPORTED(errno))
+                        return fd_is_namespace_by_name(fd, type);
+
                 return -errno;
+        }
 
